    {
      "type": 1,
      "content": {
        "json": "# Azure Virtual Desktop transport health\n*Select your Log Analytics workspace using the picker at the top of this workbook before applying the filters below — it is not fixed to any single environment.*\n- **Direct UDP (managed):** RDP Shortpath for managed networks - a private connection (ExpressRoute/VPN) directly between client and session host. Log Analytics `UdpUse` = 1.\n- **Direct UDP (public/STUN):** RDP Shortpath for public networks with ICE/STUN - a direct UDP connection discovered over the internet without a relay. `UdpUse` = 2.\n- **Relayed UDP (TURN):** RDP Shortpath for public networks via TURN - UDP relayed through a Microsoft-managed TURN server, typically because of symmetric NAT. `UdpUse` = 4.\n- **TCP fallback:** The session uses the supported reverse-connect TCP/TLS 443 path because no UDP Shortpath option succeeded or was reported. This can also happen if a host pool's RDP Shortpath networking settings (`ManagedPrivateUdp`/`DirectUdp`/`PublicUdp`/`RelayUdp`) disable a path by policy, or if the client blocks it via configuration (client-side group policy/RDP property `Enable RDP Shortpath for managed networks` disabled, an outdated Remote Desktop client that doesn't support Shortpath, or a local firewall blocking outbound UDP) - not only due to network conditions.\n- **Unknown:** Available diagnostics do not contain enough transport evidence; verify Connection, Network, Error, and MultiLink collection.\nCompare each transport with RTT, available bandwidth, and correlated errors before drawing conclusions. Source: [Configure RDP Shortpath for Azure Virtual Desktop](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/virtual-desktop/configure-rdp-shortpath).\n\n---\n*Created by Jonathan Mesa | jonathan.mesa@microsoft.com | Sr. Cloud Solution Architect*"
